    {
      "id": "KR_wHBvXpLpflHRdBip_a",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -4362.939527727656,
      "y": -1838.6085275348644,
      "width": 2201.458740234375,
      "height": 1100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "ymSPxk3kNTy7EXuHD_zDO",
      "index": "a2l",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1943387567,
      "version": 1434,
      "versionNonce": 1356337025,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785345549945,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "\"11 Powerful Techniques to Supercharge your ML Models\"\n\n\n#1) Robustify Linear Regression\n    > regression models are sensitive to outliers\n    > Consider \"Huber regression\" and \"Huber loss\"\n\n#2-3) Supercharge kNN Models\n    > kNN is highly sensitive to the parameter, k\n    > To address high-dimensional datasets\n        > distanced-weighed kNN\n        > dynamically update the hyperparamter k\n\n#4) Feature Discretization\n    > transform a continous feature into discrete features (one-hot features)\n        > equally sized bins\n        > equal-frequency bins\n\n#5) Label Smoothing\n    > single-label classification datasets, the entire probability mass belongs to a single class, and the rest are zero.\n        > Label smoothing is a lesser-talked regularisation technique\n\n#6) Data Shuffling\n    > Deep learning models may fail to converge due to various reasons. Some causes are obvious and common, and therefore, quickly rectifiable, like too high/low learning rate, no data normalization, no batch normalization, etc.\n        > mini-batch gradient descent (whereas batch gradient descent is the entire dataset rather than subsets)\n`In the context of label-ordered data, where samples of the same class are grouped together, mini-batch gradient descent will lead the model to learn patterns specific to the class it excessively saw early on in training.\n\nIn contrast, randomly ordered data ensures that each mini-batch contains a balanced representation of classes. This allows the model to learn a more comprehensive set of features throughout the training process.`\n\n#7) Zero-inflated regression\n    > datasets literally have plenty of zeros with little to no non-zero values to work with\n    > Training using a combination of classificaiton and a regression model\n        > mark all non-zero targets as 1 and the rest as 0\n        > train a binary classifier on this dataset\n        > train a regression model only on the non-zero true target datasets\n\n#8) Cyclical feature encoding\n\n#9) Robusitfy KMeans\n\n#10) Focal loss\n\n#11) Rank consistent classification\n    > most multi classification neural networks trained using cross entropy loss function",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\"11 Powerful Techniques to Supercharge your ML Models\"\n\n\n#1) Robustify Linear Regression\n    > regression models are sensitive to outliers\n    > Consider \"Huber regression\" and \"Huber loss\"\n\n#2-3) Supercharge kNN Models\n    > kNN is highly sensitive to the parameter, k\n    > To address high-dimensional datasets\n        > distanced-weighed kNN\n        > dynamically update the hyperparamter k\n\n#4) Feature Discretization\n    > transform a continous feature into discrete features (one-hot features)\n        > equally sized bins\n        > equal-frequency bins\n\n#5) Label Smoothing\n    > single-label classification datasets, the entire probability mass belongs to a single class, and the rest are zero.\n        > Label smoothing is a lesser-talked regularisation technique\n\n#6) Data Shuffling\n    > Deep learning models may fail to converge due to various reasons. Some causes are obvious and common, and therefore, quickly rectifiable, like too high/low learning rate, no data normalization, no batch normalization, etc.\n        > mini-batch gradient descent (whereas batch gradient descent is the entire dataset rather than subsets)\n`In the context of label-ordered data, where samples of the same class are grouped together, mini-batch gradient descent will lead the model to learn patterns specific to the class it excessively saw early on in training.\n\nIn contrast, randomly ordered data ensures that each mini-batch contains a balanced representation of classes. This allows the model to learn a more comprehensive set of features throughout the training process.`\n\n#7) Zero-inflated regression\n    > datasets literally have plenty of zeros with little to no non-zero values to work with\n    > Training using a combination of classificaiton and a regression model\n        > mark all non-zero targets as 1 and the rest as 0\n        > train a binary classifier on this dataset\n        > train a regression model only on the non-zero true target datasets\n\n#8) Cyclical feature encoding\n\n#9) Robusitfy KMeans\n\n#10) Focal loss\n\n#11) Rank consistent classification\n    > most multi classification neural networks trained using cross entropy loss function",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
